    id: 'cia',
    cls: 'institution',
    label: 'Central Intelligence Agency',
    cluster: 'ic',
    sub: 'Independent civilian foreign intelligence service; convener of the Robertson Panel',
    desc: 'The CIA was created by the National Security Act of 1947 - signed on 26 July 1947, seventeen days after the Roswell debris story was killed by the Fort Worth weather-balloon photographs - and it is the agency whose documented conduct most nearly matches the pattern this board is built to test. The Agency\'s own historian, Gerald K. Haines, published \'CIA\'s Role in the Study of UFOs, 1947-1990\' in Studies in Intelligence in 1997; the article is an eighteen-page institutional admission that the Agency ran a UFO effort out of the Office of Scientific Intelligence, convened the Robertson Panel in January 1953 under H. P. Robertson, and adopted a policy of public debunking coupled with the surveillance of civilian UFO organisations. The Robertson Panel\'s declassified recommendations - that the phenomenon be stripped of its \'aura of mystery\' through a programme of \'education\' and \'debunking\', and that groups such as the Aerial Phenomena Research Organization be monitored \'because of their potentially great influence on mass thinking\' - are the founding charter of American UFO information management. Haines additionally records the Agency\'s later position that U-2 and OXCART overflights accounted for a large share of the UFO reports of the late 1950s and 1960s, a claim that simultaneously excuses the deception and concedes that the Agency knowingly allowed false explanations to stand. The CIA maintains a public FOIA collection, \'UFOs: Fact or Fiction?\', which it characterises as containing \'CIA information on this subject from the 1940s through the early 1990s\', consisting mostly of \'cables reporting unsubstantiated UFO sightings in the foreign press and intra-Agency memos about how the Agency handled public inquiries\'. That framing is itself a claim: it asserts that the Agency\'s UFO holdings are administrative rather than substantive, and it is the assertion that every subsequent FOIA fight has contested.',
