    label: 'The Galileo Project',
    cluster: 'research',
    sub: 'Harvard-based unclassified UAP and interstellar-object instrumentation programme, founded July 2021',
    desc: 'Founded by Avi Loeb on 26 July 2021, four weeks after the ODNI preliminary assessment and following NASA administrator Bill Nelson\'s June 2021 call for scientific analysis, the Galileo Project is the largest unclassified instrumentation effort ever directed at this problem. Its founding logic is the board\'s own logic inverted: if the classified system will not release what it holds, build instruments that acquire an independent record nobody can classify. It raised roughly $1.8 million in its first year from donors including Frank Laukien of Bruker Corporation and William A. Linton of Promega, and involves over 100 researchers across Harvard, Caltech, Cambridge, Princeton, Stockholm University, Wellesley and the University of Tokyo. It operates in three branches: high-resolution multimodal observation of UAP, the search for interstellar objects resembling \'Oumuamua under Amir Siraj, and the search for possible extraterrestrial satellites. Its first observatory was installed at Harvard College Observatory in 2022. Unusually for this field, it published its instrumentation and software architecture before its observations - the Journal of Astronomical Instrumentation special issue of 2023, including Loeb and Laukien\'s project overview and Cloete et al.\'s integrated computing platform for detection and tracking - and it continues to publish auxiliary instrumentation in mainstream venues, such as the 2025 Copernicus paper on deploying a geomagnetic variometer station. Its most contested undertaking was the June-July 2023 Pacific Ocean expedition to recover fragments of CNEOS 2014-01-08.',
