    id: 'roswell-1947',
    cls: 'event',
    label: 'Roswell Incident',
    cluster: 'legacy',
    sub: 'Recovery and retraction, 7-9 July 1947',
    desc: 'In late June 1947 rancher W. W. \'Mac\' Brazel found debris scattered on the Foster Ranch near Corona, in Lincoln County, New Mexico, roughly seventy-five miles northwest of Roswell. On 6 July he reported it to Chaves County Sheriff George Wilcox, who called Roswell Army Air Field. RAAF dispatched Maj. Jesse A. Marcel Sr., intelligence officer of the 509th Bomb Group - at that date the only nuclear-capable bomb group in existence - and Capt. Sheridan Cavitt of the Counter Intelligence Corps. On 8 July 1947 the base public information officer, 1st Lt. Walter Haut, issued a press release stating that \'the intelligence office of the 509th Bomb group of the Eighth Air Force, Roswell Army Air Field, was fortunate enough to gain possession of a disc.\' The story went out on the wires within hours. That same day Brig. Gen. Roger M. Ramey, commanding Eighth Air Force at Fort Worth Army Air Field, staged a press conference with weather officer Warrant Officer Irving Newton and presented the material as a weather balloon and a radar target reflector. The Air Force\'s own subsequent reports concede the essential structure: the 1994 report identified the debris as a Project Mogul balloon train, specifically Flight No. 4 launched 4 June 1947 from Alamogordo Army Air Field, and explicitly acknowledged that the weather balloon statement had been a cover story to divert attention from a classified programme; \'The Roswell Report: Case Closed\', published 24 June 1997 and authored in part by declassification officer 1st Lt. James McAndrew, attributed accounts of recovered bodies to anthropomorphic parachute test dummies dropped in 1950s high-altitude programmes. Roswell\'s evidentiary value to this board does not depend on adjudicating what the debris was. It rests on two documented facts. First, the United States has conceded in writing that it issued a false public explanation for a materiel recovery in 1947. Second, when Rep. Steven H. Schiff asked GAO to audit the paper trail after the Defense Department declined to answer him, GAO/NSIAD-95-187 reported that RAAF outgoing messages from October 1946 through December 1949 had been destroyed and that no disposition authority for the destruction could be located. The unit\'s message traffic for the period covering the recovery no longer exists, and nobody has ever produced the authority that ordered it destroyed. That finding has never been rebutted by any agency.',
